  "Number": 7,
  "Question": "If the operation \\(x*y\\) is defined by \\(x*y=(x+1)(y+1)-1,\\) then which one of the following is false?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(x*y=y*x\\) for all real \\(x\\) and \\(y.\\)",
    "B": "\\(x*(y+z)\\) equals \\((x*y)+(x*z)\\) for all real\n\\(x,\\) \\(y,\\) and \\(z.\\)",
    "C": "\\((x-1)*(x+1)\\) equals \\((x*x)-1\\) for all real \\(x.\\)",
    "D": "\\(x*0=x\\) for all real \\(x.\\)",
    "E": "\\(x*(y*z)=(x*y)*z\\) for all real \\(x,\\) \\(y,\\) and \\(z.\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "The definition gives \\(x*y=xy+x+y\\). A statement claimed for all real inputs is false if even one allowed substitution fails. In B, take \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=z=0\\). Since \\(1*0=(1+1)(0+1)-1=1\\), we get\n\\[1*(0+0)=1,\\qquad (1*0)+(1*0)=2.\\]\nThus B is false.\n\nTo verify that it is the only false choice, consider the other statements. A holds because \\(xy+x+y=yx+y+x\\). D holds because \\(x*0=(x+1)\\cdot1-1=x\\).\n\nFor C, applying the definition to each side gives\n\\[(x-1)*(x+1)=x(x+2)-1=x^2+2x-1,\\]\nand\n\\[(x*x)-1=(x+1)^2-2=x^2+2x-1.\\]\nThus C holds.\n\nFor E, the useful form of the definition is \\((u*v)+1=(u+1)(v+1)\\), valid for all real \\(u,v\\). Applying it to the inner operation gives\n\\[x*(y*z)=(x+1)(y+1)(z+1)-1,\\]\nand\n\\[(x*y)*z=(x+1)(y+1)(z+1)-1.\\]\nThese expressions agree, so E holds as well. The false statement is choice B.",
